    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Polymeric sand colour is the one decision in a paver installation that cannot be undone without grinding the joints back out. Everything else is reversible; this is not. It is worth ten minutes of thought and a test patch.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-colours" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. The Colours Available
        </h2>

        <p>
          Names vary between brands, but the palette across the industry comes down to five families. Big-box stores usually stock the first two; anything else generally means a hardscape supplier or an online order.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Grey / Slate</h3>
        <p>
          The most-sold colour and the safest default. It reads as neutral against almost every concrete paver, disappears against grey and charcoal units, and hides everyday dirt well. On a mixed-tone paver it is usually the shade that offends nothing. Where it disappoints is against warm-toned pavers, where a cool grey joint can look faintly industrial.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Tan / Beige</h3>
        <p>
          The other mainstream option, and the right one against warm pavers: sandstone, buff, terracotta and the tan-to-brown blends that dominate suburban patios. It softens the whole surface and looks natural against planting. Against a cool grey paver, however, tan joints stand out more than most people expect.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Charcoal / Black</h3>
        <p>
          A deliberate design choice rather than a neutral one. Dark joints frame each paver crisply and give a contemporary, defined look, particularly with large-format grey slabs. They also hide dirt and organic staining well. The catch is that any haze left on the paver faces during installation is far more visible against a dark joint, so this colour is least forgiving of a rushed job.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Ivory / Light Beige</h3>
        <p>
          Striking against pale limestone or white-toned pavers, and the highest-maintenance option by a distance. Light joints show soil, moss, algae and leaf staining quickly, especially in shade or under trees. Beautiful in the right setting, and a commitment.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Brown / Chestnut</h3>
        <p>
          Less common, and useful against clay pavers, red brick and warm natural stone where tan is too pale and charcoal too severe. Availability is the constraint — this is firmly in the order-online category.
        </p>

        <!-- Colour table -->
