        <h2 id="match-or-contrast" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Match or Contrast?
        </h2>

        <p>
          This is the actual decision, and colour names only matter once it is settled.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Matching</strong> makes the paving read as a single continuous surface. The pattern recedes and the area looks larger and calmer. It is also considerably more forgiving: irregular joint widths, slightly misaligned courses and the small compromises every DIY installation contains all become much harder to see when the joints do not draw the eye.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Contrasting</strong> does the opposite. Every unit is outlined, the laying pattern becomes a feature, and a herringbone or basketweave reads clearly instead of blurring into texture. It looks deliberate and modern — and it exposes every inconsistency in the joints, because you have just drawn a line around each one.
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical rule we would give anyone laying their own patio: if the joints are even and the pattern is crisp, contrast is a strong choice. If the laying was a first attempt, or the pavers are irregular natural stone, match — the finished result will look considerably more professional than an outlined version of the same work.
        </p>

        <p>
          One more consideration that gets forgotten until the furniture arrives: the joints are seen alongside everything else in the garden, not in isolation. A charcoal joint that looks sharp against bare pavers in a photograph can read heavily once a dark table, dark planters and a dark fence are around it. Look at the wider palette of the space — fence colour, house wall, planting and furniture — before deciding, and lean neutral when several of those are already dark.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-they-age" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. How Each Colour Ages
        </h2>

        <p>
          Nothing stays the shade it was in the bag. Three changes happen, all predictable.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Curing shift.</strong> The colour changes as the binders activate and the joints dry, usually settling slightly darker and duller than the dry sand looked. This is why judging colour from the bag alone misleads people, and why a test area needs a day before you assess it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Dust and weathering.</strong> Over the first year or two, most colours drift towards a greyer, softer version of themselves as fine dust embeds in the surface of the joint. Premium brands hold their colour noticeably better than budget bags; that difference is one of the few things you are genuinely paying extra for.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Biological staining.</strong> In shade, under trees, or in damp climates, algae and moss will darken and green any joint over time. Light colours show it first and most, dark ones barely at all. If the patio is north-facing or overhung, that alone is a strong argument against ivory.
        </p>

        <p>
          One consequence worth planning for: joints in sun and joints in permanent shade on the same patio will diverge over a few years. It is not a defect, and there is no product fix — it is the same reason one end of a fence weathers faster than the other.
        </p>

        <h2 id="by-paver" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. By Paver Type
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Grey concrete pavers.</strong> Grey to match, charcoal to outline. Tan almost always looks like an accident here.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Multi-tone concrete pavers.</strong> Pick the mid-tone of the blend rather than the lightest or darkest colour in it. Matching the extreme makes the joints fight with half the pavers.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Clay pavers and brick.</strong> Brown or tan. Grey against red clay reads cold, and charcoal is usually too heavy against a warm, textured unit.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Natural stone and flagstone.</strong> Match the dominant tone, and remember that natural stone joints are usually wide — check the joint width rating on the bag before falling in love with a colour, because wide-joint formulations come in fewer shades.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Travertine and pale limestone.</strong> Ivory or light beige if you accept the maintenance; tan if you would rather it stayed looking clean with less effort.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Large-format modern slabs.</strong> Charcoal is the signature look, and it works — provided the installation is clean enough to justify drawing attention to every joint.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Matching an Existing Patio</h3>
        <p>
          Extending a patio, or re-sanding part of one, brings a harder version of the problem: you are matching a colour that has already weathered rather than one on a chart. Fresh sand in the same nominal shade will look brighter than the aged joints beside it for the first year or two, and there is no way to accelerate that.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two approaches work. The first is to accept the mismatch and re-sand the whole surface rather than a section, so everything ages together from the same starting point — on a small patio this is often less work than it sounds, and it removes the problem permanently. The second is to choose a shade slightly darker or duller than the original bag, on the reasoning that new sand lightens the comparison and time will close the gap. Neither is perfect, and the second requires a test area you are prepared to live with.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the original colour is unknown, take a chunk of cured joint sand to a hardscape supplier rather than guessing from a photograph. Cured joints photograph badly, and colour on a screen is the least reliable way to make this decision.
        </p>

        <h2 id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Testing Before You Commit
        </h2>

        <p>
          Cured polymeric sand cannot be tinted, overlaid or washed out, so the test is the whole insurance policy.
        </p>

        <p>
          The quick version: tip a little dry sand from the bag into an existing joint in an inconspicuous corner and look at it in daylight, not under a store's lighting. That catches an obviously wrong choice in two minutes.
        </p>

        <p>
          The proper version: install a square yard exactly as you would the whole patio — swept in, compacted, topped up, cleaned off and watered — and look at it the next day, in daylight and again in the evening. This is the only test that shows the real cured colour, and it also functions as a rehearsal for the watering technique, which is where most polymeric sand jobs go wrong. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-polymeric-sand/">how to use polymeric sand</a> covers that process, and <a href="/polymeric-sand-installation-mistakes/">the common installation mistakes</a> is worth reading before the first bag is opened.
        </p>

        <p>
          Choose a corner near the edge of the patio for the test, not the middle. If the colour turns out to be wrong, removing one square yard from an edge is a manageable morning; removing it from the centre of a finished surface is not.
        </p>

        <h2 id="performance" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Does Colour Affect Performance?
        </h2>

        <p>
          Not directly. What determines whether a joint holds is the binder system, the grain grading and the joint width rating on the bag — not the pigment. A charcoal premium sand and a tan premium sand from the same line will perform identically.
        </p>

        <p>
          Colour does change how visible your mistakes are, which amounts to the same thing in practice. Haze — the milky film left when binder washes onto the paver faces — shows dramatically against dark joints and can be almost invisible against light ones. Conversely, uneven joint depth is more obvious with a pale sand, because the shadow line at a low joint contrasts with the bright surface beside it.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is one genuinely practical effect: dark joints get hotter in direct sun, which on a large charcoal-jointed area of dark pavers contributes to a surface that is uncomfortable underfoot in summer. It is a small factor next to the paver colour itself, but around a pool it is worth a thought.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">A Note on Sealing Over Coloured Joints</h3>
        <p>
          Sealing a paver field after the joints have cured changes the joint colour as well as the paver colour, usually deepening both. A wet-look sealer in particular can turn a mid-grey joint noticeably darker, which occasionally converts a matched scheme into an unintentional contrast.
        </p>
        <p>
          If sealing is part of the plan, factor it into the test: seal the test square as well, and judge the combination rather than the sand alone. The sequence also matters — joints need a full cure, typically around a month, before a sealer goes over them, and our guide on <a href="/how-long-after-polymeric-sand-can-you-seal-pavers/">how long to wait before sealing</a> covers why rushing that traps moisture in the joint and causes hazing later.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging Colour From the Dry Bag</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dry sand looks lighter and brighter than the cured joint will. The colour settles darker and duller once the binders activate, which is why a test area needs a full day before you judge it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Ivory for a Shaded Patio</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Light joints in shade or under trees pick up algae, moss and leaf staining within a season or two. Beautiful in full sun on pale stone; a maintenance commitment anywhere damp.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Contrasting Over an Uneven Installation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dark joint outlines every paver, including the ones that are slightly out of line. If the laying is not crisp, matching hides far more than contrast flatters.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing Bags of Different Colours</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bags can differ in grain and binder loading as well as pigment, and a mixed shade is impossible to reproduce if you run short. Buy one colour, in one order, plus a spare bag.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Falling for a Colour the Joint Width Rules Out</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wide-joint formulations come in fewer shades than standard ones. Measure the joints before choosing a colour, or you will end up either with the wrong product for the gap or a different shade than planned.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What colours does polymeric sand come in?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Grey or slate, tan or beige, and a darker charcoal or black, with ivory and brown from some brands. Big-box stores usually stock only grey and tan; wider palettes come from hardscape suppliers or online, which matters if you have set your heart on an unusual shade.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Should the sand match or contrast?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Matching makes the paving read as one surface and hides irregular joints; contrasting outlines each unit and emphasises the pattern. Match on a large area or an imperfect installation, contrast on a crisp pattern with even joints.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the colour fade?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">It shifts rather than fades. Most colours settle towards a greyer, duller version over the first year or two as dust embeds and weather works on them, with darker shades showing the change least. Premium brands hold colour noticeably better than budget bags.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which colour hides dirt best?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Mid-tone greys and tans. Very light shades show soil, moss and organic staining quickly, especially in shade. Very dark shades hide dirt well but show dust, pollen and lime bloom more obviously.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does colour affect performance?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No — the binder system and the joint width rating decide performance, not the pigment. What colour changes is how visible your mistakes are: haze on the paver faces shows far more against dark joints, and uneven joint depth shows more with a pale sand.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you mix colours?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Not advisable. Bags can differ in grain size and binder loading as well as pigment, and a mixed shade cannot be reproduced if you run short mid-job. Buy one shade in one order, with a spare bag.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you choose without seeing it laid?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Tip a little dry sand into a joint in a corner and look at it in daylight for a quick check. Better, install a proper square-yard test — swept, compacted, cleaned and watered — and assess it the next day, because the colour changes as the binders cure.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you change it later?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Only by removing it. Cured polymeric sand cannot be tinted, and fresh sand swept on top will not bond to the old joint. Changing colour means grinding or chipping the existing joint sand out to depth — slow work, and rarely worth it unless the original choice is badly wrong.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

